Since I didn't experimented everything on the game yet, I'll put only what I used/discovered so far:
- History: Didn't finished yet. It's a standard history, easy to understand.;
- Soundtrack (In races) - Exciting music!;
- Soundtrack (Free roam) - I THINK there's less than 20 tracks, since I keep hearing the same often. ALSO NO ROCK, C'MON UBISOFT!;
- Controls:
-- Keyboard: Standard racing game, only a bit harder when driving on Dirt roads;
-- Gamepad: Stil didn't tested;
-- LT G27: Still not discovered the most realistic settings for it, but it's good so far. Also, you have to hit the clutch even when using Sequential gear change. Don't know if it's like that on real cars. Of course, you have Automatic and H-Shifter as well;
- Interface: Veeeery very easy to access everything;
- Map: It's USA, what do you think? It's freaking huge. Good thing you can fast travel via Trains/Airplanes or in car if you already discovered the location (You can Fast Travel with cars to every single point on the map, not a specific Airport/Station);
- Cars: There's about 30 including the unlockables.
-- Customization: Some cars have all 5 types (Street, Dirt, Perfec(?), Circuit and other I don't remember now) and each one have nice customization options (Dirt cars remembers Power Rangers Turbo a lot);
-- Paintings: Plenty. You'll definitely find one that suits you;
-- Interior painting: Bad. Baaaaaaad. Only 5 colors (Red, Black, Yellow, Blue, White) and 4 types (Whole Seat, Seat Specific Regions, Car Panel and some combinations);
That's it for now. I'll put more things the more I play.

After more than two years dreaming of playing
boiled down to less than 4 hours of lackluster gameplay, I found
an impossible game to
recommend without a handful of 'howevers'.
Thomas is alone. You guide him through supposedly scarring imagery and haunting monsters towards answers that speak more about himself than the world he conjured. Thomas, it seems, is a tad bit out of his mind and, in turn, tries to drag you into the depths with him.
The problems? To summarize, this game is
. Most (if not all) the horror elements are classic in white people western mainstream horror culture; this game sports but is not limited to:
• Your usual white woman with straight black hair;
• Dolls;
• Asylums;
• Abandoned victorian architecture;
• Westernized paintings hanging from wallpapered walls;
• A graveyard full of "Johns" and the like and, as far as I saw, one Japanese name... Sakura;
• Sourceless random laughter and crying (voiced by women because why men?);
• The Holy Bible;
• Blonde bloke and
• Striped pajamas.
These things, by themselves, are by no means a problem. However, consider: eastern games played on Steam
boil down to half-explicit graphic novels and MMOs. When people drill constant western horror imagery into our heads trough movies, games, books and the like, it gets very very hard to even be bother by it because most of us, westerns, are exposed to nothing else.
I am not a tough cookie. I did get startled a couple of times, but that was that.
didn't leave me speechless because I was shocked or horrified, it left me speechless because I just couldn't take in that was all it was.
Neverending Nightmare's level design is weak. A few sections of the levels are just gorgeous and ingenious and fantastically crafted and detailed; most of it is the same thing you saw a few minutes ago. That gets boring, that gets annoying, that gets confusing, that gets frustrating. That's saying plenty, since the game is so short.
There are a few strong reasons why I still recommend
despite its several weak points:
• It is visually
;
• It leaves more questions than answers and the possibility to speculate rather than impose;
• Voice acting, soundtrack and sounds, despite generic, are well done and even better with a headset;
• What it promises, it delivers;
• It has full controller support;
• It has achievements;
• It has trading cards and
• Most people will not be bothered by the fact they will be fed their usual diet.
Neverending Nightmares is another horror game that barely saves itself from being just that by having a pretty face and an open mind about what it chooses to showcase.
